# The Periodic Table: Order from Atomic Number

> Sixty-three known elements defied order until Mendeleev arranged
> them by mass — and Moseley later corrected the key to atomic
> number. The table's shape now predicts how every element behaves.

## 1. From Triads to the Modern Law

| Attempt | Scientist (Year) | Idea | Why it fell short |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Triads | Döbereiner (1817) | middle element's mass ≈ mean of the other two (Li 7, Na 23, K 39) | only a handful of triads worked |
| Law of octaves | Newlands (1865) | every 8th element resembles the first, like musical octaves | broke down after calcium |
| Periodic table | Mendeleev (1869) | elements ordered by atomic mass; gaps left for the unknown | a few elements seemed out of order |
| Atomic number | Moseley (1913) | X-ray spectra expose the true ordering number | — (basis of the modern table) |

[definition]
The **modern periodic law**: the properties of elements are a
periodic function of their **atomic number** (proton count), not
their atomic mass.
[/definition]

[example]
Mendeleev's boldest move was leaving gaps for elements not yet
found. He predicted **eka-aluminium** (gallium, found 1875) and
**eka-silicon** (germanium, 1886) — with startlingly accurate
masses and properties. The fulfilled predictions won the day.
[/example]

## 2. Anatomy: Groups, Periods, and Blocks

@diagram tree id="periodic-table-blocks" layout="tree" aria="Periodic table divided into s, p, d and f blocks"
  "Periodic table" -> "s-block: groups 1-2, plus helium"
  "Periodic table" -> "p-block: groups 13-18"
  "Periodic table" -> "d-block: groups 3-12 (transition metals)"
  "Periodic table" -> "f-block: lanthanides and actinides"
@/diagram

- **Groups** — 18 vertical columns; members share a valence
  configuration, hence similar chemistry.
- **Periods** — 7 horizontal rows; each fills one outer shell
  (lengths 2, 8, 8, 18, 18, 32 track subshell capacities).
- **Blocks** — named for the subshell being filled: s, p, d, f.

## 3. The Families

| Group | Family name | Members |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | Alkali metals | Li, Na, K, Rb, Cs, Fr |
| 2 | Alkaline earth metals | Be, Mg, Ca, Sr, Ba, Ra |
| 3–12 | Transition metals | Fe, Cu, Zn, Ag, Au, ... |
| 16 | Chalcogens | O, S, Se, Te, Po |
| 17 | Halogens | F, Cl, Br, I, At |
| 18 | Noble gases | He, Ne, Ar, Kr, Xe, Rn |

The **inner transition metals** — the **lanthanides** (Z = 58–71)
and **actinides** (Z = 90–103) — fill f orbitals and are pulled out
below the main table.

[tip]
Group 1 memory sentence: *Little Naughty Kids Rub Cats Fur* —
Li, Na, K, Rb, Cs, Fr. For the halogens: *Fine Cool Brides Ignore
Actors* — F, Cl, Br, I, At.
[/tip]

## 4. Trends Across the Table

| Trend | Across a period (→) | Down a group (↓) | Reason |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Atomic radius | decreases | increases | nuclear charge pulls inward; new shells expand |
| Ionization energy | increases | decreases | electron lies closer to / farther from nucleus |
| Electron affinity | generally increases | generally decreases | sharper pull toward a near-complete shell |
| Electronegativity | increases | decreases | fluorine tops the scale at 4.0 |
| Metallic character | decreases | increases | metals live at the bottom left |

