[warning]
Two famous dips: Be → B falls because boron's new 2p electron sits
farther out than its 2s; N → O falls because oxygen's fourth 2p
electron must pair up, adding repulsion to a stable half-filled
shell.
[/warning]

## 5. Special Elements and Exceptions

- **Fluorine** — most electronegative element (4.0).
- **Noble gases** — highest ionization energies in their periods;
  filled shells make them chemically aloof.
- **Helium** — the smallest atom; placed in group 18 because its
  1s² shell is complete.
- **Mercury** — the only metal liquid at room temperature;
  **gallium** melts in your hand at 29.8 °C.

## 6. Diagonal Relationships

[definition]
A **diagonal relationship** links an element with its down-right
neighbour — Li–Mg, Be–Al, B–Si. Similar sizes and charge
densities give the pairs unexpectedly similar chemistry.
[/definition]

| Pair | Shared traits |
| --- | --- |
| Li and Mg | both burn to nitrides; carbonates decompose on heating |
| Be and Al | both form amphoteric oxides and covalent halides |
| B and Si | both form acidic oxides and covalent hydrides |

## 7. Quick Revision

| Question | Answer |
| --- | --- |
| What orders the modern table? | atomic number (Z) |
| Who left the famous gaps? | Mendeleev — eka-silicon became Ge |
| Alkali metals? | group 1: Li Na K Rb Cs Fr |
| Halogens? | group 17: F Cl Br I At |
| Left to right, radius...? | decreases |
| Most electronegative? | fluorine, 4.0 |
| Diagonal pairs? | Li–Mg, Be–Al, B–Si |
